-## 9. Spec-replay results so far and choice of B (round 3)
-
-Fg decode tok/s (sum predicted_n / sum predicted_ms, greedy), from
-results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay/{off,A}-greedy/requests.jsonl:
-
-| phase | off | A (ngram-mod defaults) | A draft_n / accepted | fg length-finishes off/A |
-|---|---|---|---|---|
-| single-agent | 55.1 | 86.0 | 8229 / 6212 | 1 / 3 |
-| single-mem | 55.6 | 53.1 | 740 / 69 | 0 / 0 |
-| dual-agent | 26.3 | 25.5 | 1856 / 259 | 0 / 2 |
-| dual-mem | 25.0 | 23.8 | 740 / 69 | 0 / 0 |
-
-A helps agent traffic with one slot busy (some of that may be looping outputs hitting
-max_tokens: 3 length-finishes vs 1) but not with both busy, and it is slightly negative
-on mem. Acceptance is low in the dual and mem phases (the default drafts 48-64 tokens per
-step, so a miss wastes a large verification batch).
-
-B = ngram-mod with `--spec-ngram-mod-n-min 8 --spec-ngram-mod-n-max 16 --spec-ngram-mod-n-match 12`:
-same algorithm, shorter lookup and much shorter drafts, to cut the cost of rejected drafts
-when both slots share the GPU, and to start drafting on shorter repeats. I keep one type
-so the difference from A is only the draft-size knobs. (ngram-map-k/simple were not
-chosen: their 12/48 defaults have the same long-draft cost.) Not yet measured.
+## 9. Full greedy spec-replay results (off / A / B, round 4)
+
+All numbers are fg decode tok/s = `sum(predicted_n) / sum(predicted_ms)` per phase,
+from `bench/results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay/{off,A,B}-greedy/requests.jsonl`. Each
+phase ran on a FRESH server (the n-gram pool persists across requests). Per-phase
+server logs are `server_<phase>.log` in each dir; per-phase `nvidia-smi` is in `vram.txt`.
+
+| phase | off | A (ngram-mod 48/64/24) | A excl-length | B (ngram-mod 8/16/12) | B excl-length |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| single-agent | 55.1 | 86.0 | 54.3 | 53.4 | 55.0 |
+| single-mem | 55.6 | 53.1 | 53.1 | 50.7 | 50.7 |
+| dual-agent | 26.3 | 25.5 | 26.1 | 23.0 | 23.0 |
+| dual-mem | 25.0 | 23.8 | 23.8 | 22.4 | 22.4 |
+
+**Excluding length rows the story inverts for A.** A's 86.0 single-agent tok/s is
+entirely from three `finish_reason=length` rows; with them out A is 54.3, which is
+*below* off's 56.2 (off's 1 length row, r1/2/8, is already length, so off's 55.1
+is also slightly depressed). On single-mem A (53.1) is below off (55.6). On the dual
+phases A is *slower* than off even after dropping its length rows (dual-agent 26.1 vs
+26.3 fg aggregate / 25.5 vs 26.3; dual-mem 23.8 vs 25.0). B is below off in every phase
+after excluding its single length row (dual-agent 23.0 < 26.3; dual-mem 22.4 < 25.0).
+
+**Draft totals per phase (draft_n / accepted, acceptance %):**
+- A: single-agent 8229/6212 (75.5%), single-mem 740/69 (9.3%), dual-agent 1856/259
+  (14.0%), dual-mem 740/69 (9.3%).
+- B: single-agent 2757/1194 (43.3%), single-mem 592/142 (24.0%), dual-agent 1562/679
+  (43.5%), dual-mem 624/145 (23.2%).
+
+A's high single-agent acceptance (75.5%) is itself concentrated in the two degenerate
+loops (server log `print_timing` lines: task 6380 `3717 accepted / 3828 generated` for
+mem0-r2/0/2; task 4350 `2135 accepted / 2242 generated` for mem0-r0/0/2). The rest of
+A's requests have per-task acceptance mostly < 0.40.
+
+**fg length-finishes, and what off did on the same id in the same phase:**
+- off: only `single-agent baseline-r1/2/8` (1323 tok, `length`).
+- A single-agent: `r1/2/8` (1323), `mem0-r0/0/2` (3121), `mem0-r2/0/2` (4854).
+- A dual-agent: `r1/2/6` (768), `r1/2/8` (1323).
+- B single-agent: `r1/2/8` (1323).
+
+Cross-check against off, same phase:
+- A dual-agent r1/2/6 -> off finished `tool_calls` at 270 tok (A looped to 768 = max_tokens floor).
+- A dual-agent r1/2/8 -> off finished `tool_calls` at 435 tok (A looped to 1323).
+- A single-agent mem0-r0/0/2 -> off finished `tool_calls` at 1020 tok (A looped to 3121).
+- A single-agent mem0-r2/0/2 -> off finished `tool_calls` at 3701 tok (A looped to 4854).
+- `r1/2/8` single-agent `length` in both off and A (off 1323, A 1323) -- consistent.
+
+So A turned 4 of its 5 length rows into degenerate max_tokens loops where off finished
+normally with `tool_calls`. These spec-induced loops are a **quality regression**, not a
+speedup; they are the entire source of A's apparent single-agent gain. Counting them as
+speedup inflates A to 86.0 tok/s; excluding them removes the gain entirely.
+
+**Output identity vs off** (same phase, same id, same fg slot, share of identical
+`out_sha256`):
+- single-agent: A 3/30 (10%), B 2/30 (7%).
+- single-mem: A 10/12 (83%), B 10/12 (83%).
+- dual-agent: A 0/30 (0%), B 0/30 (0%) (expected -- batch mix differs without a lossless guarantee).
+- dual-mem: A 11/12 (92%), B 11/12 (92%).
+
+Off-vs-spec output identity **cannot** be assumed from lossless verification: even with
+zero drafts, enabling speculation changes the temperature-0 output. `single-agent
+baseline-r0/0/0`: off sha `530c...` (71 tok, draft_n 0/None) vs A sha `cdad...` (71 tok,
+draft_n 0). Both 71 tokens, no drafting, different text.
+
+**Checkpoint evidence in logs.** Neither A nor B logs contain any `restore checkpoint`
+(server-context.cpp:3813) or `speculative decoding will use checkpoints`
+(server-context.cpp:1172) lines -- the latter is `SRV_TRC` and the former is not emitted
+at the replay's default verbosity (server logged `verbosity = 3`). At this verbosity the
+spec activity shows only as `print_timing: ... draft acceptance = X (N accepted / M
+generated)` lines. So the logs alone do not distinguish checkpointed from non-checkpointed
+speculation here; the per-request `draft_n`/`draft_n_accepted` in `requests.jsonl` (non-zero
+for A and B) is the authoritative acceptance measure. Counts: `restore checkpoint` = 0 in
+all A and B phase logs.
+
+**mmproj outcome.** No `--mmproj`/`--no-mmproj-offload` fallback fired for off, A or B
+(no `-nommproj` dirs; `notes.txt` ends in `done`). All spec arms ran with mmproj
+loaded and produced `draft_n > 0`, so **ngram self-speculation works with mmproj enabled
+on text-only traffic**. This matches the corrected §3 verdict and the standing facts:
+the server-context.cpp:3550-3553 `has_embd + spec` refusal only bites multimodal/embedding
+batches, which this replay is not. (The replay is text-only.)
+
+**B caveat.** B's `n_match=12` triggered the server warning
+`spec common_specu: ngram_mod n_match=12 is too small - poor quality is possible`
+once per phase (in `B-greedy/server_*.log`). B's short-draft setting was thus flagged as
+poor-quality risk by the server itself, and it also underperforms A on every fg phase.
+
+**Prod-arm selection (for the prod-sampling runs).** By the item's dual-agent greedy
+dual-sload rule, A (25.5) beats B (23.0); excluding the capped rows A (26.1) still beats
+B (23.0). So the prod arm is **A** (`--spec-type ngram-mod` defaults) -- i.e. the next
+runs are `off-prod` and `A-prod`. Note that this selection is driven by A merely being
+the *less-bad* of two spec configs; A is still slower than off in the dual phases, so
+the selection is for completeness, not for deployment.
+
+**Running conclusion (feed to the deploy decision).** The decision question -- "does
+ngram self-speculation speed up real traffic with both slots busy?" -- the answer on these
+numbers is **no**. In *both* dual phases the spec-off config is the fastest: dual-agent
+off 26.3 > A 25.5 > B 23.0; dual-mem off 25.0 > A 23.8 > B 22.4. With one slot busy, the
+only apparent gain (A single-agent) is an artifact of spec-induced max_tokens loops
+(quality regression); excluding them A is at or below off. Spec also changes temperature-0
+outputs even at zero drafts, so it is not lossless. B's settings were server-flagged as
+poor quality and it loses to A. **Decision: do not deploy.** See §10 for the deployment
+rule and the final writeup.
+
